# default.map — the shipped strictness profile: RISK CATEGORY → review roles added to the floor.
#
# Why this file exists. The tier used to decide BOTH the composition of the review set and its depth,
# through a hardcoded three-branch `case` in delivery-lib.sh. So the five risk categories the classifier
# already computes (bin/select-pipeline.sh: security/auth, data/schema, infra/deploy, api/contract, deps)
# collapsed into one tier word and were discarded into `reasons=` — a ready signal with no addressee.
# Composition now comes from the categories; the tier keeps only depth.
#
# This is the Spec Kit preset model: a profile OVERRIDES the mapping, it never adds capability. An
# organisation ships its own file and leaves the core untouched. Override the path with
# $TEAM_BOOTSTRAP_PROFILE.
#
# Format: `<category><space/tab><role> [role...]`; `#` comments and blank lines ignored.
#
# TWO RULES THIS FILE CANNOT BREAK, enforced in tests/roles-alive.test.sh:
#   * ADD-ONLY. Entries are unioned into the tier-derived base set. A profile can never shrink a set the
#     paths already earned, and the >=1 independent-reviewer anti-collapse floor is never sized away.
#   * NO DEAD ENTRIES. Every role named here must have an agents/<slug>.md (or it cannot be dispatched)
#     AND a required verdict field in references/schemas/role-output.schema.json (or it cannot be
#     confirmed at closure). Every category key must be one select-pipeline.sh actually emits.
#
# NOT MAPPED, on purpose — api/contract. Its natural owner is integration-verifier, but the category
# itself forces the `full` tier, whose base set ALREADY contains that role. The mapping would therefore
# never change an outcome: `bin/eval-role.sh --liveness` reports such a binding DEAD, because removing it
# turns nothing red. A no-op entry inflates the appearance of routing without adding any, so it is gone.
#
# NOT MAPPED, on purpose — a hot-path/performance category. performance-reviewer is dispatchable and
# typed, but no path pattern honestly answers "is this a hot path": a guessed classifier would produce a
# binding that fires on the wrong batches, and `eval-role.sh --liveness` would rightly call the guess
# alive while it routed noise. It stays reachable by DECLARATION — `⚠ performance-reviewer` on the task —
# which required_roles_for_batch already unions in, one-directionally. A real signal (profiling data, a
# benchmark budget) can add the category later; a fabricated one cannot be improved, only unwound.
#
# NOT MAPPED, on purpose — test-designer. Same reason as chaos-engineer: no required field in the schema,
# so it cannot be confirmed at closure.
#
# NOT MAPPED, on purpose — chaos-engineer, test-designer, legal-compliance-checker. None declares a
# required field in role-output.schema.json, so none can be CONFIRMED at closure: assigning them buys a
# dispatch and no confirmation, which is the decoy the whole verdict pipeline exists to reject. Giving
# any of them one is a new required handoff field = a MAJOR bump (references/versioning.md), a decision
# rather than a detail. infra/deploy is served by devops-platform instead, which IS typed (ci_status)
# and denies Write/Edit, so it satisfies the anti-builder invariant in review mode.
#
# NOT MAPPED, on purpose — ip-contracts-reviewer. It IS typed (ip_verdict) and read-only, but its
# frontmatter declares `compatible_pipelines: [audit-dd]`, and the orchestrator refuses a pipeline
# containing an incompatible role. Routing it into a full/mvp code batch would violate the role's own
# declared contract; widening that list is a role-version decision, not a routing one.

security/auth   security-reviewer
data/schema     data-schema-reviewer
deps            security-reviewer overengineering-reviewer
ui              accessibility-reviewer
infra/deploy    devops-platform
perf            performance-reviewer
